# Defence in depth for the Cloudflare deploy. The primary fix is the build command — see below.
#
# Cloudflare parses any `_redirects` it finds in the asset directory and rejects Netlify's
# dialect outright, failing the deploy at the Deploying step after a clean build:
#
#   Line 10: Valid status codes are 200, 301, 302 (default), 303, 307, or 308. Got 0.   (`301!`)
#   Line 71: Valid status codes are 200, 301, 302 (default), 303, 307, or 308. Got 404. (`404`)
#
# That is what failed on 2026-10-12 and 10-16. `_redirects` is generated for Netlify; on
# Cloudflare the Worker does the routing itself (worker/index.ts), so the file is not merely
# invalid there, it is unnecessary.
#
# PRIMARY FIX: Cloudflare's build command must be `npm run build:cloudflare`, which deletes the
# file outright (scripts/strip-cloudflare-incompatibles.ts). The deploy failed because that
# command was not set, so the strip step never ran.
#
# This file is the backstop for that setting being wrong again. Vite copies it into dist/ on
# every build. NOTE: its effect could not be verified locally — `wrangler deploy --dry-run` does
# not upload assets, and its "Read N files" count is taken before filtering, so it reports the
# same number with or without any pattern here. Wrangler 4.141 does implement
# CF_ASSETS_IGNORE_FILENAME = ".assetsignore", and Cloudflare's own framework scaffolding writes
# `public/.assetsignore` for the same purpose, so this is expected to work on a real deploy —
# but treat the build command as the fix that is known to work, not this.
_redirects
